WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:03.160
This video was made possible by Curiosity
Stream.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:06.919
When you sign up at the link in the description
you’ll also get access to Nebula—the streaming

00:00:06.919 --> 00:00:10.440
video platform that Wendover is a part of.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.220
There are few sports as expensive as skiing.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:19.179
A daily lift-ticket alone typically costs
more than $100 with top resorts, such as Vail,

00:00:19.179 --> 00:00:22.470
charging up to $209 for a day’s skiing.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.269
It’d be easy to call this a rip-off.

00:00:25.269 --> 00:00:31.099
After all, all those $209 are getting you
is access, along with thousands of other people,

00:00:31.099 --> 00:00:35.650
to one of 32 lifts between the hours of 8:30
am and 3:30 pm.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:39.680
Does it really cost that much for a resort
to run a few lifts?

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.920
Well, the short answer is sort of.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.050
Ski lifts are a simple enough concept.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:49.461
With the most common design, chairs, each
with a capacity between one and eight, are

00:00:49.461 --> 00:00:54.160
connected to a continuously rotating cable,
pulled by motors at the top or bottom of the

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:55.160
lift.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:00.190
Between all the redundancies and safety systems,
though, they end up being fairly complex machines,

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:02.140
and complex means expensive.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:07.540
While it varies wildly depending on length,
capacity, specifications, and location, costs

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:13.540
for the construction of a new lift alone regularly
reach $10 million, and most large ski resorts

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.630
have dozens of lifts.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:18.800
In Vail’s case, once again, they have 32.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.720
That means that, even if they were all the
kinds of lifts that cost $10 million, which

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:26.680
they’re not, that would total to $320 million.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:32.750
Those $320 million are divided by a lift lifespan
of a few dozen years, then divided further

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:36.760
by the hundreds of thousands of yearly visitors
who are each paying up to hundreds of dollars

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:42.470
per day, and you can start to see pretty quickly
that it is not because of building lifts that

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:43.820
skiing is expensive.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:48.381
The paradox of ski resorts is that buying
and installing ski lifts, which are their

00:01:48.381 --> 00:01:54.350
primary physical assets, only represents a
small proportion of their costs.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:58.689
Actually running ski lifts is trickier, but
for a slightly unexpected reason.

00:01:58.689 --> 00:02:02.840
Between working the line, scanning tickets,
helping riders load, and manning the terminal

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:07.680
to stop the lift in case of an accident, a
good number of workers are needed to operate

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:08.840
each lift.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:13.080
Then, when you add in all the restaurants,
ticket sales, rental shops, stores, hotels,

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:18.121
activities, and everything else a large resort
has, it needs thousands of employees, but

00:02:18.121 --> 00:02:21.890
getting such huge numbers of workers is quite
tough.

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:26.400
The nature of the majority of these jobs is
that they’re low-wage and seasonal.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.550
That means that a resort needs to find a subset
of people who are willing to move to the mountain

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.280
for five or six months and work for not much.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:37.920
There are not many people who want to do that
and, among those who are, they're typically

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.830
young people who will only work a season or
two before entering a longer-term career.

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:47.800
Running a ski resort is an enormous and never-ending
recruiting mission and many are unable to

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.920
fill all their positions, even when touting
significantly better benefits than most other

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:57.110
entry-level jobs, at least in the US, like
healthcare, transit passes, 401k’s, and

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:58.460
free ski passes.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:03.510
This labor scarcity has pushed wages up for
ski jobs, but even with higher wages they

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.349
run into another issue—housing.

00:03:06.349 --> 00:03:10.450
The nature of the ski resort economy is that
the towns that ski resorts are in need to

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:15.920
be able to accommodate a seasonal doubling
or tripling or quadrupling of their population.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.390
Couple that with the fact that resorts tend
to be nestled in the mountains where there

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:24.170
is little flat or semi-flat land where one
can build cheap housing, and the urban geography

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.500
of these areas is wacky.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.989
Take the example of Aspen.

00:03:28.989 --> 00:03:33.610
Aspen is home to four ski areas, all operated
by the Aspen Skiing Company, and therefore,

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:39.209
in the winter, the greater Aspen area is also
home to thousands of seasonal workers.

00:03:39.209 --> 00:03:44.390
Many of these workers are paid the company’s
base wage of $13.50 an hour, however, Aspen

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:48.970
also happens to be one of the world’s most
expensive real estate markets.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:53.980
The reason for that is, as usual, a byproduct
of supply and demand.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:57.700
Housing supply is quite low as the town is
nestled at the end of a mountain valley with,

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.319
in the winter, only one road in or out.

00:04:00.319 --> 00:04:03.040
Housing supply for small apartments is even
lower.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:07.050
The average price per square foot of a home
in Aspen is nearly $1,700.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:13.040
In comparison, in Manhattan, New York, it’s
under $1,400.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:17.630
Of course, no matter how much they pay, Aspen
Skiing Company isn’t going to get workers

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:19.910
unless they have somewhere to live.

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:24.440
While prices decrease as one descends down-valley,
for the most part, there would be no chance

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:29.650
of housing thousands of low-wage workers using
the free market, so Aspen Skiing Company’s

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:34.330
solution is to provide subsidized employee
housing at prices that all their workers can

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:35.330
afford.

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:40.750
So, while a ski resort may be able to pay
their employees $13.50 an hour, their employees

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:43.560
cost them quite a bit more than that.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:48.220
There is, however, another unique byproduct
of the ski-town labor market.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:52.560
The masses of seasonal workers often don’t
bring cars with them, so they need ways to

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:53.560
get around.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:59.340
Now, the US is not known for its public transportation
systems, and it’s known even less for its

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:05.150
rural public transportation systems, but American
ski towns are often an exception to this.

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:09.560
Many have public-transportation systems that
rival those of big cities.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:14.340
Colorado, home to many of the US’ most popular
ski resorts, leads the nation in terms of

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:17.470
ridership on its rural transportation systems.

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:22.530
Of these, the system with the highest ridership
is Aspen’s, with more than 5 million passengers

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:23.530
per year.

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:28.630
That’s remarkable for a town of 7,000, even
though the system extends far beyond, and

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:34.729
its core routes have busses every 12 minutes
or more—again, rivaling many routes in big

00:05:34.729 --> 00:05:35.729
cities.

00:05:35.729 --> 00:05:40.820
This system, much like those of all of Colorado’s
ski towns, is crucial to get workers in and

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:45.670
out of the area and it represents the symbiotic
relationship ski resorts and the towns they’re

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:47.800
located in often have.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:51.740
Ski resorts need their ski towns to set themselves
up in a way that’s accommodating for their

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:56.680
workers and guests, while ski towns need ski
resorts in order to stay ski towns.

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:03.441
But here’s something strange: most big ski
resorts, at least in the US, don’t actually

00:06:03.441 --> 00:06:05.540
own the land they operate on.

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:10.210
It’s government land, and the resorts only
hold permits to use the land.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:16.680
This means that the land upon which one skis
is public land and so, with some restrictions,

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:20.920
anyone can use the land regardless of whether
or not they have a lift ticket.

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:26.360
At American resorts sitting on US Forest Service
land, of which there are 122, people legally

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:30.900
can and do hike uphill and then ski down without
a pass.

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:36.240
Therefore, technically, what the resorts are
selling is access to the lifts themselves,

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:37.770
not the mountains.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:42.460
Despite this, though, people obviously wouldn’t
pay to ride those lifts up unless the skiing

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:47.139
down was good, so resorts spend huge amounts
of money building and maintaining the downhill

00:06:47.139 --> 00:06:48.139
runs.

00:06:48.139 --> 00:06:51.750
Some of this only needs to happen once a year,
like trimming any trees trying to grow in

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:56.460
the middle of runs in summer, while some needs
to happen every night.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:02.270
Snow quality can vary a lot and, without attention,
can get bad quickly, so therefore, every night

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:06.220
snowcats go out to groom the easier and more
frequented runs.

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:10.590
But sometimes, grooming isn’t enough to
keep snow quality good.

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:14.610
Sometimes, there just isn’t enough snow.

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:18.610
The one solution to that, aside from waiting,
is snowmaking.

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:23.800
There are many different designs of snow guns
but they all essentially work the same way—they

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:26.780
spray out tiny droplets of water that quickly
freeze.

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:31.600
Of course, that still requires temperatures
to be below freezing, but this is common at

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:34.360
nighttime in even more temperate areas.

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:39.449
Some resorts, especially smaller ones in warmer
locations, rely almost fully on artificial

00:07:39.449 --> 00:07:44.340
snow, while larger resorts use it to extend
their season or build up snowpack on high-use

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:45.340
areas.

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:47.660
There are, though, disadvantages to this.

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:52.300
From the ski resort’s perspective, the main
one is that it’s hugely expensive.

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:56.620
Constructing a system over a vast mountain
area is expensive, sourcing the large quantities

00:07:56.620 --> 00:08:02.540
of water needed is difficult, and then the
process itself is extremely energy intensive.

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:07.410
Exact costs vary, given the number of variables
involved, but in general, snowmaking enough

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:12.220
to cover an acre of terrain costs an average
of $5,000.

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:15.590
That means that, to fully cover one of the
largest resorts, like Whistler-Blackcomb,

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:22.780
in snow, it would cost more than $40 million,
and of course, all of that can melt in a few

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:23.920
warm days.

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:29.280
In practice, though, none of the larger resorts
rely so heavily on artificial snow, at least

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:35.300
right now, but snowmaking still represents
a huge cost for resorts large and small.

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:40.659
The snowsports business is, understandably,
one of the most threatened industries by climate

00:08:40.659 --> 00:08:43.289
change and the rising temperatures it can
bring.

00:08:43.289 --> 00:08:48.040
Now, the projected effects of climate change
on mountainous areas are complicated.

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:52.200
It’s not as simple as saying that temperatures
are going to be higher and therefore it will

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:53.350
snow less.

00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:58.239
In some places, the change in global climate
will actually bring more precipitation and

00:08:58.239 --> 00:08:59.850
therefore more snow.

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:02.279
In most, though, it’s the opposite.

00:09:02.279 --> 00:09:07.360
Lower-altitude and more southern resorts especially
will see their season lengths shrink and shrink

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:08.360
and shrink.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:12.970
Eventually, at many of these, the number of
days they stay open will not be enough to

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:17.499
pay the bills, especially as the use of expensive
snowmaking increases.

00:09:17.499 --> 00:09:21.670
Higher-altitude and more northern resorts
will actually likely benefit from this, at

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:26.679
least in the short-term, as more people travel
to them faced with fewer alternatives.

00:09:26.679 --> 00:09:30.829
Looking at what’s to come, the industry
has become cognizant of the storm ahead and

00:09:30.829 --> 00:09:34.579
started to make changes to address its primary
threat.

00:09:34.579 --> 00:09:39.569
While some are physical changes—like building
more snowmaking capacity—most are changes

00:09:39.569 --> 00:09:41.850
to the way they do business.

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:46.230
Money can overcome nature, at least for a
while, so what they're doing is making sure

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:48.399
the money’s still coming in.

00:09:48.399 --> 00:09:54.499
Consequently, the North American ski industry
has seen massive consolidation in recent years.

00:09:54.499 --> 00:09:59.610
This trend only started in earnest five years
ago when Vail Resorts, the company that owns

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:01.410
Vail, went on a spending spree.

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:05.120
In 2015, the company owned 11 resorts.

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:09.310
Now, five years later, they own 37.

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:14.569
The link this has to climate change is that
now, Vail Resorts, which is a public company,

00:10:14.569 --> 00:10:18.970
has properties spread out across different
climates so, for example, if there’s a bad

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:22.889
snow year in the Rockies, they always have
properties in British Columbia, where the

00:10:22.889 --> 00:10:24.209
snow might be better.

00:10:24.209 --> 00:10:31.100
They even own resorts in Australia so Vail
spins lifts somewhere on earth nearly year-round.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:36.110
Not to be outdone, Aspen Skiing Company, which
runs the four famous Aspen-area resorts, teamed

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:41.209
up with KSL Capital Partners, a private equity
firm, in 2018 and formed a joint venture to

00:10:41.209 --> 00:10:44.119
compete against Vail: Alterra Mountain Company.

00:10:44.119 --> 00:10:49.129
Alterra now owns a more modest 15 resorts
across the US and Canada.

00:10:49.129 --> 00:10:53.369
With the resorts they own and partner with,
both of these companies released offerings

00:10:53.369 --> 00:10:57.119
that are changing the financial formula of
the ski industry.

00:10:57.119 --> 00:11:01.920
For Vail, it’s the Epic Pass; for Alterra,
it’s the Ikon pass; but for the both of

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:06.790
them, these are essentially season passes
that, instead of working at just one place,

00:11:06.790 --> 00:11:09.850
work at dozens of resorts worldwide.

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:15.290
This is a killer competitive move against
independent resorts since, in many ways, these

00:11:15.290 --> 00:11:18.799
are better for the consumer and better for
the company.

00:11:18.799 --> 00:11:23.670
The consumer gets variety and flexibility
at prices not much different than single-resort

00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:29.560
season passes, while the companies are selling
more season passes which is exactly what they

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:30.560
want.

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:34.059
That’s because season passes hedge risk.

00:11:34.059 --> 00:11:38.449
Even though they’ll make more money in a
day from someone buying a single-day pass,

00:11:38.449 --> 00:11:41.870
those buying those day passes are highly weather
dependent.

00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:46.619
If there’s tons of snow, they’ll sell
tons of day passes, if there’s little snow,

00:11:46.619 --> 00:11:48.549
they’ll sell barely any.

00:11:48.549 --> 00:11:52.920
Nobody wants their quarterly earnings reports
to depend too much on the weather, especially

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:57.749
as it’s getting worse, and so, by getting
more of their customers onto season passes,

00:11:57.749 --> 00:12:01.500
which are typically sold before the start
of the season when there are few indications

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:06.009
of the season’s snowfall, the consumer is
sharing the risk with the company, and revenue

00:12:06.009 --> 00:12:08.980
variability year-to-year will lower.

00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:14.029
This is a crucial shift for the ski companies
as year-to-year snow variation is expected

00:12:14.029 --> 00:12:15.809
to only pick up.

00:12:15.809 --> 00:12:21.749
So, in all, the real shift the ski industry
is having to make is not how they run their

00:12:21.749 --> 00:12:24.879
slopes, but rather how they make money.

00:12:24.879 --> 00:12:30.309
For them, while they still might lobby, fundraise,
and rally against it, they’re now a point

00:12:30.309 --> 00:12:35.120
where reality is imminent, and climate change
is all but another component of their financial

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:38.059
plans.

00:12:38.059 --> 00:12:42.100
If you want a little bit more of the action
side of snow-sports, and a little bit less

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:47.389
of the business side, believe it or not, Curiosity
Stream has something for you to watch next.

00:12:47.389 --> 00:12:52.259
The series, “Jeremy Jones: Higher,” follows
professional snowboarder Jeremy Jones as he

00:12:52.259 --> 00:12:56.559
attempts to be the first person to snowboard
down sections of the Himalayas, and shows

00:12:56.559 --> 00:13:01.249
everything that goes on behind the scenes
of an expedition as huge as that.

00:13:01.249 --> 00:13:06.170
This goes to demonstrate just how diverse
